You have a great eye for this. Yes, there are exactly **two other places** in the controllers we built where a generic catch (Exception e) is acting like a black hole and swallowing your specific validation messages.
If we don't fix these, the frontend will show generic "Failed" messages instead of telling the user exactly what went wrong.
Here are the two spots to fix:
### 1. In VoucherActionController.java (Delete Batch)
Currently, if the user tries to delete a batch but a specific business rule blocks it, the controller catches it in a generic Exception block and overrides the message with *"Failed to delete batch"*.
**Find deletePendingBatch and replace it with this:**
```java
    @DeleteMapping("/my-requests/by-batch/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponseDto> deletePendingBatch(@PathVariable String batchId, @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) {
        try {
            commandService.deleteBatchRequest(batchId, jwtUtil.getUserIdFromToken(token.substring(7)));
            return ResponseEntity.ok(new ApiResponseDto("Success", "Batch deletion process initiated.", null));
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN).body(new ApiResponseDto("Error", e.getMessage(), null));
        } catch (CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException e) {
            // FIX: Catch specific business errors and send the exact message to React
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(new ApiResponseDto("Error", e.getMessage(), null));
        } catch (Exception e) {
            log.error("BATCH_DELETE_FAIL | Batch: {} | Error: {}", batchId, e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body(new ApiResponseDto("Error", "An unexpected error occurred.", null)); 
        }
    }

```
### 2. In VoucherQueryController.java (Download Excel)
If the user tries to download an Excel file for a batch that has no records, your service explicitly throws CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException("No records found for batch."). But the controller catches it and returns a completely empty 400 Bad Request with no body!
**First, change the return type of the method from ResponseEntity<byte[]> to ResponseEntity<?> so it can return either a file OR a JSON error message.**
**Find downloadBatchExcel and replace it with this:**
```java
    @GetMapping("/download-batch/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<?> downloadBatchExcel(@PathVariable String batchId, @RequestHeader("Authorization") String token) {
        try {
            if (!queryService.isUserAuthorizedForBatch(batchId, jwtUtil.getUserIdFromToken(token.substring(7)), jwtUtil.getUserRoleFromToken(token.substring(7)))) {
                return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.FORBIDDEN).body(Map.of("message", "You are not authorized to view this batch."));
            }
            
            byte[] fileData = queryService.generateBatchExcel(batchId);
            org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders headers = new org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders();
            headers.setCacheControl(org.springframework.http.CacheControl.noCache().mustRevalidate().sMaxAge(java.time.Duration.ZERO)); 
            headers.set(org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders.CONTENT_DISPOSITION, "attachment; filename=\"Batch_" + batchId + ".xlsx\""); 
            headers.set(org.springframework.http.HttpHeaders.CONTENT_TYPE, "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet");
            
            return ResponseEntity.ok().headers(headers).body(fileData);
            
        } catch (CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException e) {
            // FIX: Return the exact "No records found" message as JSON
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST).body(Map.of("message", e.getMessage()));
        } catch (Exception e) { 
            log.error("DOWNLOAD_ERROR | Batch: {} | Reason: {}", batchId, e.getMessage());
            return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body(Map.of("message", "Failed to generate Excel file."));
        }
    }

```
### Why this matters:
By explicitly catching CustomExceptions.InvalidDataException before the generic Exception e catch block, we preserve the exact text you typed into your backend rules. Your React frontend's catch block will now safely extract those messages and display them in the Snackbar just like the file upload!
